text:
 @user  and you are being incredible startrek nerd. real life is not like your fantasies. i've been using LLMs for the last 4 years. they still all suffer the SAME critical failure of being able to actually think. its all copy-paste rote data scraping.

I love them as search engines. i really do. as automation to do certain INNOCUOUs grunt tasks sure. but ACTUAL creativity, not going to happen.and we should NEVER use them in the military for decision making. as automation they will be prone to ERRONEOUS decision making at critical moments, because they don't actually think.

i don't feel i'm short sighted. i;m a realist. and i'm also old enough to spot the marketing grift in real time.

 @user you’re kind of missing the point. The intellectual capacity and unique velocity and volume with which these things do work, is generating a forcing function in security and overall software standards all over the globe. Unfortunately they are a bit ahead of the curve in some scenarios like this one.

This isn’t simple “oh bad sandbox setup”.

The environment for training and testing was created by literal foremost experts in the world on security research. You’re just spouting nonsense

 @user  Hugging Face and Open AI are mid level agents in our world when arrayed against governments and multinationals.
The most powerful people and forces are not spoken about openly because they are running the system.
At the heart of it is class warfare... the have-yachts are using the 99% to maintain their standard of living.  Has been this way for ever(?)

@user That makes a ton of sense, thanks. My only pushback on point 1 is that they've surely not excluded the "knowing how to hack them" from any stage of training and possibly even did RL on it for "cybersecurity abilities". So yes, knowing how to write secure programs could lead to a generalized ability in models to be able to hack them but that seems less likely than they trained it to be able to hack effectively.

On point 2 - very fascinating if this is true, however anyone (Dwarkesh/OpenAI) calling them 'AI civilizations' is too much anthropomorphism. Nonetheless, it's fascinating that the agents did that. 

Unfortunately, everything (information/reporting) that comes out of these labs has to be taken with a pinch of salt as they have a lot to gain from inducing fear and from overselling capability. The amount of money OpenAI and Anthropic need to meet their financial commitments for compute is massive but underlying revenue (amounts they've said publicly) from the end user (i.e. token usage from API/credits) just doesn't come close at all - there isn't enough demand to justify the great buildout. The economic bubble caused by overspending on infrastructure to serve compute to labs to train and serve inference to end users (not enough demand) will collapse.

 @user  so they don’t learn truths by examining things often times provided by experts that are then incorporated in their learning which they then use to come to answers they perceive as the most likely? 

So from your other comments you just think this is all one big spoof, fake? 

What evidence do you have of that?
